---
title: Challah
servings: 12
yield: 1 braided loaf, 16 inches
tags: [jewish, holiday, vegetarian, dairy-free]
cuisine: jewish
time: 3 hours 50 minutes
source: Classic Challah by PJ Hamel, King Arthur Baking, https://www.kingarthurbaking.com/recipes/classic-challah-recipe
---

Mix the @all-purpose flour{480%g}, @water{170%g}(lukewarm), @vegetable oil{74%g}, @honey{63%g}, @eggs{2}, @egg yolk{1}(keep the white), @table salt{9%g} and @instant yeast{12%g} in a #large bowl{}.

Knead to a soft, smooth dough. If it stays very sticky, work in up to 30 g more flour.

Cover and leave ~{2%hours}, until puffy. It may not quite double.

Deflate it gently on a lightly greased counter and divide it into 3, 4 or 6 pieces, depending on the braid. Roll each into a rope about 20 inches long, resting them ~{10%minutes} if they shrink back.

Braid the ropes and lay the loaf on a #baking sheet{}, greased or lined with parchment. Cover with greased plastic wrap and leave ~{60%minutes} to 90 minutes, until very puffy.

Near the end of the rise, heat the oven to 375°F (190°C) with a rack in the upper third.

Beat the reserved egg white with @water{1%tbsp}(cold) and brush it over the loaf. Scatter with @sesame seeds{}(or poppy seeds, optional).

Bake ~{25%minutes} to 30 minutes, until deep golden and the middle reads 190°F (88°C). Cool on a #wire rack{}.

> For a round Rosh Hashanah loaf, roll all the dough into one 36-inch rope,
> coil it in a greased 9-inch cake pan, let it rise and bake the same way.

> To bake in the morning, braid it the night before, cover and refrigerate, then
> give it 60 minutes at room temperature before baking.
